================================================================================
mpi-gpu IRREGULAR-PAIR SWAP: portable auto-vectorized sources as default
================================================================================
Date     : 2026-06-11
Branch   : mpi-parallel (follows G0, results_g0_mpi_gpu_2026-06-11.log)
Host     : gpudyn3 -- A40, CUDA 12.5, 16-core AVX-512 Xeon, Open MPI 4.1.2
Binary   : GPU2/run_ampere/nbody7b.mpi-gpu (MPIGPU_IRR=cpu, the new default)

WHAT CHANGED (acts on G0 finding 5)
  GPU2/Makefile: the mpi-gpu CPU-side irregular-force PAIR -- the irregular
  library and the c.m. neighbour kernel travel together -- now defaults to
  the portable C++ sources written for the Mac CPU/Metal port:
      irrlib/gpuirr.cpp + lib/cnbint6.cpp        (MPIGPU_IRR=cpu, default)
  which gcc -O3 -march=native auto-vectorizes to the host's widest ISA.
  The original hand-written SSE-intrinsics pair remains selectable for
  pre-AVX Intel CPUs (their vintage):
      irrlib/gpuirr.sse.cpp + lib/cnbint.cpp -> cnbint5.cpp  (MPIGPU_IRR=sse)
  The pairs differ at bit level (summation width/order), so the np1-vs-npK
  bit-identity guarantee holds WITHIN a pair, not across them. `make clean`
  required when flipping MPIGPU_IRR (cp -p object aliasing).
  mpi_design/README.md: mpi-gpu build/run section added.

ACCEPTANCE (same suite as G0, new binary)
  run_equiv.sh  : PASS -- np1/2/4 bit-identical, all ranks. Smoke banners:
    "device 0: NVIDIA A40" + "Opening GPUIRR lib. CPU ver." (the CPU-pair
    identifier; the SSE pair prints "SSE ver.") on every rank.
  run_io_guard.sh : PASS -- np4 shared dir byte-identical to np1 reference.
  N=5e4: np2/np4/np8 ADJUST + END RUN physics bit-identical to np1 (both
    cadences at np8).

MEASURED EFFECT (N=5e4, wall s, rank 0; "sse pair" columns = G0 log)

  asis                  cpu pair          sse pair (G0)
                      np1     np8         np1     np8
  P3  irr slice       9.14    1.60       27.35    2.25
  P7  reg slice       0.22    1.77        0.26    1.78
  A2  ADJUST tot      8.83   15.65        8.78   15.59
  P12 OUTPUT          8.13    8.21        8.11    8.41
  SUM                31.22   35.76       52.35   36.16

  np sweep, cpu pair (asis SUM): np1 31.2, np2 29.5, np4 30.5, np8 35.8
  adj4x: np1 20.8, np8 27.7 (sse pair: 37.0 / 27.7)

FINDINGS
  1. The swap delivers exactly the predicted 3x on P3 at np1 (27.3 -> 9.1 s)
     and drops the np1 total 52.3 -> 31.2 s (asis) / 37.0 -> 20.8 s (adj4x).
  2. THE STORY FLIPS ON A 1-GPU NODE: np1 now beats np8 (31.2 vs 35.8 s).
     With P3 only ~9 s, decomposing it (saving ~6 s at np8) no longer pays
     for the replicated-sweep DRAM contention inside ADJUST (A2 8.8 ->
     15.6 s) plus GPU sharing (P7 0.22 -> 1.77 s). The sweep minimum is a
     shallow np2 (29.5 s, ~6% below np1); np1-np4 essentially flat.
  3. Net best config on gpudyn3 at N=5e4: mpi-gpu np2 = 29.5 s -- 5.6x vs
     cpu np1 (164.7 s) and 1.7x vs the best mpi-cpu (np8, 51.2 s). For
     practical purposes the plain np1 GPU run is just as good here.
  4. Where MPI ranks WILL pay on the GPU build: multi-GPU nodes (one rank
     per device -- the binding is in place, untested), larger N (P3 grows
     ~linearly while A2 contention grows with np, not N), and production
     ADJUST cadences once the A2 residue is trimmed. The G2 priority from
     G0 stands, sharpened: ADJUST-internals (A2-A1 ~ 15 s at np8, ~9 s of
     it np-invariant bookkeeping at np1) and OUTPUT (8 s) are the wall.
